WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.197
Fewer calls, and calls that overlap.

2
00:00:02.477 --> 00:00:11.437
The first term of the equation multiplies TTFT by the number of calls, and in an agent each call also adds the time spent between calls.

3
00:00:11.717 --> 00:00:14.043
Two families of fixes: Fewer calls.

4
00:00:14.323 --> 00:00:18.547
Combine sequential steps into one prompt when the model can do them together.

5
00:00:18.827 --> 00:00:28.235
Prefetch what your application already knows the agent will need (the customer’s account, the order) and put it in the first request instead of waiting for a tool call.

6
00:00:28.515 --> 00:00:30.669
Make list tools return more per page.

7
00:00:30.949 --> 00:00:34.064
In the cookbook, prefetching cut calls per task from 3.

8
00:00:34.344 --> 00:00:35.368
5 to 2.

9
00:00:35.648 --> 00:00:40.619
4 and median time by 13 percent; bigger pages cut it by 10 percent.

10
00:00:40.899 --> 00:00:42.392
Overlap the calls you keep.

11
00:00:42.672 --> 00:00:45.531
Claude can request several tool calls in one turn.

12
00:00:45.811 --> 00:00:57.757
Run independent, read-only tools concurrently, and return all the results together in a single user message (splitting them across messages teaches the model to stop calling in parallel).

13
00:00:58.037 --> 00:01:07.872
Run independent subtasks as concurrent requests, each with its own model and effort: the cookbook’s parallel Haiku subagents cut one task from 9.

14
00:01:08.152 --> 00:01:09.261
5 to 5.

15
00:01:09.541 --> 00:01:11.781
7 seconds with the same decisions.

16
00:01:12.061 --> 00:01:28.531
Two cautions: concurrency raises cost when each subtask rereads its own context, and parallel requests with identical prefixes don’t share a cache until the first one has started responding, so fire one, wait for it to start streaming, then fire the rest.
